/Pg 10 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
145 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 111 0 R
/E (5G development requires high network capacity, low latency, and massive connectivity. However, conventional Radio Access Network \(RAN\) infrastructure, which is closed and monolithic, has become a bottleneck due to vendor lock-in. This has driven the industry to shift toward a more open, intelligent, and interoperable architecture: O-RAN.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
146 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 111 0 R
/E (The O-RAN paradigm, which prioritizes an open and disaggregated architecture, introduces new security risks by expanding the attack surface on open interfaces such as E2.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
147 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 111 0 R
/E (This openness exposes the network to fatal attacks targeting Availability \(service availability\), such as Signaling Storms and Denial of Service \(DoS\), which are difficult to detect because they exploit standard RAN protocols.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
